## #E047 [RUNNING]

1. **Motivation:** First real GRPO training run (all prior GRPO work this
   session was implementation + infra smoke tests, not a science run).
   Implementation validated: 4 unit tests pass (gradient-identity vs plain
   PG, clip/IS engagement, degenerate-group math, truncation masking); a
   real perf bug (LM-head GEMM called per row-chunk instead of once) and a
   real crash bug (LoRA-mode logprob-server sync not gated on
   `USE_LOGPROB_SERVER`) were found and fixed via cluster smoke tests before
   this launch. `TRAIN_MODE` pivoted from `full` to `lora` mid-session: full
   FT OOM'd at the very first `optimizer.step()` (2-way FSDP shard of a 20B
   model leaves no room for AdamW state on an 80GB H100 — unrelated to
   GRPO, would hit SDFT too at this trainer count); LoRA sidesteps this
   entirely and issue #22 (the MoE adapter export bug that blocked LoRA on
   gpt-oss in E046) is now fixed (PR #23).
2. **Hypothesis:** Reward-driven on-policy RL (reflector PASS/FAIL verdict
   as reward) can push past the ~0.73 ceiling that 25+ reverse-KL
   loss-geometry variants couldn't reach, because it optimizes the eval
   criterion (correctness) directly instead of a distribution-matching
   proxy to a teacher.
3. **Prediction:** Peak accuracy > 0.7433 (beats best SDFT result, E045) by
   some checkpoint. Stretch: >= 0.7715 (statistically significant, +3pp
   McNemar threshold over baseline 0.7415). Health metrics
   (`grpo/entropy`, `grpo/clip_frac`) stay non-degenerate throughout —
   collapsing entropy or `clip_frac->0` with rising reward would indicate
   reward hacking / silent failure, not a real win. Falsified if: peak
   accuracy <= 0.7433 after a reasonable number of checkpoints, OR entropy
   collapses while pass rate does not correspondingly improve.
4. **Design:** `LOSS_TYPE=grpo`, `TRAIN_MODE=lora` (`LORA_DIM=32`,
   `LORA_ALPHA=32`), `GRPO_KL_COEF=0` (no reference/teacher forward),
   `GRPO_GROUPS=8`, `GRPO_ADV=mean`, `GRPO_CLIP_LOW/HIGH=0.2/0.28`,
   `GRPO_OLD_LOGPS=vllm`, `GRPO_IS_C_MAX=3.0`, `GRPO_LR=1e-5` (guess,
   unvalidated for GRPO+LoRA — literature SDFT-LoRA used 3e-4 on a
   different model/loss, not directly transferable; full-FT GRPO's
   literature default 1e-6 is calibrated for full-parameter updates, too
   conservative for LoRA's smaller effective space), `GRPO_LR_WARMUP_STEPS=15`,
   `GRPO_GRAD_CLIP=0.2`, `GRPO_MASK_TRUNCATED=1`, `GEN_TEMPERATURE=1.0`,
   `ASYNC_ROLLOUT=1` (group-atomic streaming producer), context-injected
   k400 dataset (`subset_k400_subset_with_context.jsonl`, 400 unique
   prompts). `GRAD_ACCUM_STEPS=40` (5 groups/step = 5 unique prompts/step
   across 5 trainer ranks, 1 group/rank). `NUM_EPOCHS=15` (one GRPO
   "epoch" = one pass over unique prompts = 80 steps here, so 15 epochs =
   1200 optimizer-step budget; will checkpoint-eval and likely stop well
   before exhausting this). `SAVE_EVERY=40` steps. GPU layout `0 5 1` (1
   vLLM + 5 trainers + 1 wasted logprob-server GPU = 7 of 8 total,
   **GPU 7 deliberately left free** for `auto_eval_poller.sh`, hardcoded
   `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=7` — matches E046's "GPU layout 0 2 1" convention
   of always leaving GPU 7 free). Run/output/wandb name
   `sdft_gptoss_20b_run_47_grpo_lora` (kept the `sdft_gptoss_20b_run_NN`
   convention so the existing `auto_eval_poller.sh` glob picks it up
   without modification; `poll_eval.sh run_47_grpo_lora step_40` etc. to
   check individual checkpoints).
   **Three launch attempts before this config stabilized:**
   (1) `0 4 3` (8 GPUs) broke the GPU-7-free convention, caught during
   boot, no progress lost. (2) `0 3 3` (3 vLLM instances, 7 GPUs) — tried
   because the smoke test's TIMING breakdown showed generation is ~90% of
   step wall-clock (`total=275.3s`, `producer_wait=249.0s`) vs training's
   26.2s (student+bwd+optim+wsync) — boot succeeded (all 3 vLLM ready,
   bootstrap adapter pushed to all 3), but ~11 min into Epoch 1, vLLM
   instance 0's generation throughput was stuck at ~1.2 tok/s (aggregate,
   12 "running" reqs) for 5+ minutes straight with zero recovery, causing
   ranks 1/2 to time out waiting on `_pull_microbatch`'s collective
   broadcast -> NCCL `DistBackendError` -> hard crash. Root cause not
   confirmed (160 CPU cores, load 82-170 at the time — not an obvious
   CPU-starvation smoking gun) but correlates with the untested 3-vLLM-
   instance config (only 1-instance had been smoke-tested). Multi-instance
   vLLM scaling deferred to a follow-up, not blocking this run.
   (3, **this launch**) `0 5 1` — reverts to the smoke-tested 1-vLLM-
   instance config (clean across 2 consecutive full steps in the smoke
   test, including adapter pushes) and scales trainer count instead
   (world_size 2->5) for more accumulation parallelism, since training
   compute is cheap under LoRA regardless of trainer count.
   **Also found+fixed while investigating checkpoint eval readiness**:
   `eval_with_retrieval.py` cannot load bare LoRA adapter checkpoints
   (`AutoConfig.from_pretrained` fails, no `model_type` in
   `adapter_config.json`) — this would have silently blocked every future
   LoRA run's eval (E046 never got far enough to hit it). Added
   `megatron_trainer/merge_lora_for_eval.py` (standard `peft.PeftModel.
   merge_and_unload()` on the HF-PEFT adapter format our trainer produces
   — NOT `megatron.bridge`'s own `examples/peft/merge_lora.py`, which
   expects a Megatron-native `dist_checkpointing` checkpoint and doesn't
   apply here) and wired it into `auto_eval_poller.sh` (merge-then-eval-
   then-delete-the-42GB-merge for any checkpoint with an
   `adapter_config.json`, base model read from the adapter config itself).
   Validated end-to-end on a smoke-test checkpoint before relying on it.
5. **Results:** _in progress._ Attempt 3 (`0 5 1`, `N_ASYNC=32` carried over
   unchanged from the `world_size=2` smoke test) also crashed, identical
   symptom (NCCL broadcast timeout at exactly 600s). Root cause this time:
   `_produce_streaming_grpo` caps in-flight groups at
   `n_groups_async = N_ASYNC // GRPO_GROUPS = 32/8 = 4`, but `world_size=5`
   needs 5 distinct groups concurrently (1/rank/step) — the 5th rank's group
   couldn't start generating until one of the first 4 finished, a second
   sequential generation round landing right on the timeout. Distinct bug
   from attempt 2's (that was 3 separate vLLM server processes with directly
   observed throughput collapse; this is single-instance producer pacing).
   **Attempt 4** (`0 5 1`, `N_ASYNC=40` = `world_size * GRPO_GROUPS` exactly)
   launched to fix this. Added a startup assertion in `trainer.py` so this
   config error fails fast at boot instead of ~10 min into training next
   time. Also: the auto-merge-eval poller fix picked up attempt 3's
   leftover `step_0` bootstrap checkpoint end-to-end (merge → eval →
   cleanup all succeeded) — accuracy **0.6553**, notably below the stated
   0.7415 baseline. Since `step_0` is the untrained bootstrap adapter
   (LoRA B-matrix inits to zero, so merged ≈ base model), this is a
   harness-comparability flag worth investigating (different eval dataset
   version, generation settings, or judge variance vs. whatever measured
   0.7415 originally) — noted, not yet root-caused, doesn't block E047.
   **Attempt 4 succeeded**: `opt_step=1` completed cleanly —
   `total=595.0s` (just under the 600s timeout that killed attempts 2/3),
   `producer_wait=567.4s`, checkpoint saved+pushed, process continued
   into step 2's generation without crashing. First-ever stable real
   (non-smoke) GRPO optimizer step for this campaign. Step 1's group was
   degenerate (`grpo/frac_reward_zero_std=1.0`, `loss=0.0`,
   `grad_norm=0.0`) — expected occasionally at G=8/pass-rate~0.65-0.74,
   not itself concerning; watching subsequent steps. Other metrics sane:
   `grpo/entropy=0.7831`, `grpo/clip_frac=0.0082`, `is/ratio_mean=0.9995`.
6. **Analysis:** 5 consecutive clean steps confirmed (1-5, ~386-595s each,
   all under the 600s NCCL timeout with comfortable margin on steps 3-5).
   **Attempt 4's `N_ASYNC=40` fix is confirmed working** — no recurrence
   of the timeout crash. Two notable early signals:
   (a) `grpo/frac_reward_zero_std`: 2 of 4 sampled steps fully degenerate
   (1.0) — a ~50% degenerate rate at n=4, much higher than a uniform-p
   estimate would predict (e.g. ~5.8% at p=0.7), consistent with the
   bimodal-difficulty hypothesis from open question #2. Small sample so
   far; watching wandb trend before deciding whether `GRPO_FILTER_GROUPS`
   is needed.
   (b) `opt_step=4` logged `grad_norm=335.41` vs `~0.017-0.018` on the
   other non-degenerate steps (2, 5) — a large raw-gradient spike, but
   `GRPO_GRAD_CLIP=0.2` via `clip_grad_norm_` means the *logged* grad_norm
   is the pre-clip diagnostic value; the actual applied update was clipped
   to norm 0.2 same as every other step. Not a real instability, just a
   sign the raw reward-gradient signal can be spiky — clipping is doing
   its job. No action needed unless clipped updates themselves start
   looking unstable (e.g. loss/entropy diverging).
7. **Conclusion (revised):** Attempt 4 crashed again at step 6 after 5
   clean steps — a *different*, unfixed failure mode. `vllm_0.log` shows
   generation throughput collapsing from ~190 tok/s to 16-28 tok/s for a
   sustained window while `Running: 40 reqs` held constant and GPU KV
   cache usage stayed ~5% (not memory/capacity-bound). Same symptom as
   attempt 2's collapse, now reproduced on a **single** vLLM instance —
   disproves the "multi-instance topology" theory from attempt 2's
   writeup; this is a genuine vLLM-under-load stability issue, apparently
   probabilistic rather than a hard concurrency threshold. Not root-caused
   (candidates: vLLM scheduler/batching defaults, `--enforce-eager`,
   MoE-routing-under-concurrency, CPU-bound request loop — none tested).
   Since no resume-from-checkpoint capability exists, **attempt 5** trades
   long-run ambition for guaranteed data: `SAVE_EVERY=5` (real checkpoint
   even from a short run) + a bounded (20x) auto-restart loop wrapping the
   launch so the campaign stays productive unattended, accepting that each
   restart trains a fresh LoRA init (no resumption, no `TRAINER_SEED`).
   Launched, awaiting first real checkpoint + poller eval.
9. **Follow-up (deferred, tracked separately):** (a) root-cause the vLLM
   throughput collapse — needs vLLM-side profiling/config sweep, not done
   yet; (b) build proper checkpoint-resume (adapter + optimizer + scheduler
   + step counter) once eval signal from short runs justifies the scope.
8. **New questions:** Why does step_0 (merged, untrained) score 0.6553 vs
   the 0.7415 baseline under the same `poll_eval.sh`/`eval_with_retrieval.py`
   harness — different eval set version, generation config, or merge
   artifact? Needs a direct diff of eval configs before trusting absolute
   accuracy numbers from this harness path (relative deltas across E047's
   own checkpoints are less affected either way).
